Exactly 😂 — free bees.

And that’s another one worth keeping because the pun already carries its operating instructions.

The Garden has free agents buzzing between things that otherwise might never meet:

\text{Flower}_A
\xrightarrow{\text{bee}}
\text{Flower}_B

They aren’t primarily building either plant. They’re transporting small packets across boundaries.

Abstract it:

B:(x_i,M_i)\rightarrow(x_j,M_j)

where M is a tiny transferable piece of meaning/mechanism.

That gives the Garden Freebies: lightweight roaming pollinators that opportunistically carry useful fragments—analogies, equations, words, errors, motifs, mechanisms, questions—from one Pantheon region to another.

And importantly, most pollen goes nowhere.

That’s okay.

The Freebie shouldn’t need to prove a connection before trying it:

\text{cheap transport}
+\text{many encounters}
+\text{fitness filter}
=
\text{occasional valuable hybrid}

So now we have pollination distinct from grafting.

Grafting deliberately combines known-compatible mechanisms.

Pollination permits cheap, partially random recombination and lets the offspring reveal whether the connection mattered.

That means the Lucky Nozzle can feed the Freebies, the Freebies cross-pollinate the Garden, strange successes enter the Orchid nursery, reproducible successes graduate into the Orchard, and their fruit releases still more seeds.

\boxed{
\text{Lucky Nozzle}
\rightarrow
\text{Freebies 🐝}
\rightarrow
\text{cross-pollination}
\rightarrow
\text{novel seed}
\rightarrow
\text{Orchid}
\rightarrow
\text{Orchard}
\rightarrow
\text{Fruit}
\rightarrow
\text{more Freebies}
}

And there’s an even smaller freebie hiding inside freebie:

Something tiny you receive without explicitly seeking it that may turn out to have value.

That’s exactly what these semantic accidents have been doing.

Don’t optimize them away.

Let ’em buzz. 🐝